WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:10.950
BRUNO AZIZA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.783
This is Bruno Aziza.

00:00:11.783 --> 00:00:14.600
And welcome to another
edition of "Data Journeys."

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.490
This is where you come
to learn from leaders

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:18.920
in data and
analytics, people that

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.950
have had amazing growth,
incredible results, who tell us

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:22.970
about their journey--

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.600
also, what we need to do and
what we need to avoid doing.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.160
Today, I've got the honor to
be here with Joe from Telus.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.910
Joe, thank you so much
for taking the time

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.600
to talk to us today.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.212
Tell us a little
bit about Telus.

00:00:35.212 --> 00:00:36.170
JOE BETTRIDGE: Perfect.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.430
Thanks for having me, Bruno.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.600
So I work at Telus.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.360
Telus is the third-largest
telecommunications company

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.050
in Canada.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.375
We represent wireless,
wireline systems.

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:47.000
We also have some
interesting divisions

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.340
in health, agriculture, and IoT.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.130
Our wireless division
represents nine and 1/2 million

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.790
subscribers across Canada.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.900
So that's a fairly large sample
of people within the country

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.130
of 36 million.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.190
And my group, specifically,
is Telus Insights.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.530
Our division was
founded in 2015.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.870
And we're really focused on
trying to commercialize data

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.460
in a privacy-preserving manner.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.220
So that means taking data
that we have as a company

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.020
and bringing it out
to market in a way

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.420
that the privacy of the
individuals or the devices

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.600
that the data comes from isn't
being impacted negatively

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.710
by anything we do.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.660
And everything is
looking at aggregates.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.640
We really see this as
a market differentiator

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.350
compared to other
data monetization

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.060
groups at other companies.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.310
And we've seen this lead
to a lot of the success

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:29.397
with our product to date.

00:01:29.397 --> 00:01:30.980
BRUNO AZIZA: That's
excellent-- so how

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.710
to monetize data
in a secure manner.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.000
You also have amazing scale.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.790
Tell us a little
about the volume

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.280
of data that you're dealing
with and the types of inputs

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.120
that you're getting.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:41.953
JOE BETTRIDGE: Yeah.

00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:43.820
So our primary data
set we focus on

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.980
is our wireless
subscriber location--

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.020
so it's location coming
off of our network.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.025
We get network events
any time a cell phone

00:01:50.025 --> 00:01:50.900
talks to the network.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.300
So it sends a signal when
it makes phone calls, text

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.820
message, as it migrates
between towers,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.550
or anytime it's been inactive
for about 30 minutes or so.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.990
And so with that, we're building
about 74 billion location

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.340
records every month.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.660
And we have about 275,000 unique
points of interest on the map

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.970
that we split up into about
5,000 different unique

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.570
polygons.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:13.190
And that number of
polygons continues

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:16.460
to grow month over month as more
users start using our system

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.823
and upload their own
custom areas to look at.

00:02:18.823 --> 00:02:20.240
And this is the
result [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.458
analyzing over 1.2
petabytes of data this year.

00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:26.000
BRUNO AZIZA: That's
quite incredible.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.375
So the products
involved here are

00:02:27.375 --> 00:02:31.280
BigQuery, of course,
Dataflow, Pub/Sub, Composer,

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.170
and a whole host
of other products.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.810
You also analyze
data in Data Studio.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.240
Tell us a little bit
about the first use case

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.610
here that you've been
primarily involved in,

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.747
is the data for good use case.

00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:43.580
JOE BETTRIDGE: Yeah.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.760
So when the pandemic hit
in mid-March in Canada,

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.620
we said as an
organization, there's

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:50.180
something we need
to do about this.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.030
And we need to provide
data out in a way that

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.580
reiterates our stance in
the market towards privacy,

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.310
so really focuses on what we
can and can't do with data,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.830
but also gives access to
that data to government

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.750
organizations and
researchers who

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.050
have a real need and a
desire to use data to inform

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:08.990
their decision making and help
them make the right decisions

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:09.800
at the right time.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.840
So we opened up to this concept.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.550
Our CEO signed off on it.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:16.200
Our chief privacy officer--
chief data and trust officer,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.870
I should say-- was really
backing this and supporting it.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.870
And so we've managed
to, in three weeks,

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:24.380
roll out a new
platform on top of GCP

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.270
that really allowed
us to provide

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.480
these insights and information
to our government customers.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.518
We signed up 26
customers-- or 27, now--

00:03:32.518 --> 00:03:35.060
since the pandemic started and
have been able to provide them

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.610
with information that
can help them understand

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.280
mobility patterns, what
communities are interconnected,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.770
what health regions are
interconnected, how far are

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.630
people traveling
on an average day,

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.490
how well are people
receiving public health

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.400
messaging, and doing all of
this with large aggregate data

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.307
samples so that nothing's
about individuals.

00:03:51.307 --> 00:03:53.390
It's all about communities
and how communities are

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:54.590
interacting with each other.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.900
And this has resulted in
some really positive effects

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.480
for various health groups
across the country, which is

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.920
something we're very proud of.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.570
And from doing this, we
actually won the HPE-IAPP 2020

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.040
Innovation in Privacy award.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.450
So that's a very
big deal for us,

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.190
and we're very excited
to have won that.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:12.410
It really shows that
privacy differentiator

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.230
is core to our product and
really core to what we do.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.480
And it's really helped
us gain traction

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.008
with these government agencies.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:21.050
BRUNO AZIZA: Well, first
of all, congratulations.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.270
And then it's amazing.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.400
Thank you for doing
this for the community.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.300
You're providing a great
service to everyone.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.440
Now, you have been at
Telus for nine years,

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.350
and you've had a
ton of experiences.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.230
I want to step back and
take a look at, overall,

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.910
why did you choose
Google, and what

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.310
has been the improvement
you've seen compared

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.158
to, maybe, on-prem systems?

00:04:42.158 --> 00:04:44.450
And then we're going to go
into the dos and the don'ts.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.227
But first, why Google for you?

00:04:46.227 --> 00:04:47.060
JOE BETTRIDGE: Yeah.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.900
So before Google, we were trying
to analyze this data using

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.540
an on-premise Hadoop cluster.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.420
What it was-- it tried
to be powerful enough.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.190
We continued to
expand that cluster.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.500
It was getting very
costly to do so.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.790
We were seeing results--

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.370
to understand something like,
maybe, a tourism region's

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.380
visitorship was taking
us about a month

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.610
to produce a month
worth of information.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.600
So we were running
at that bleeding edge

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.440
where, if we got
one more data point,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.870
we wouldn't hit our
deadlines for customers.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:18.010
So we were getting in this
very concerned mode of working,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.208
where it wasn't right.

00:05:19.208 --> 00:05:21.250
And we knew there was
something better out there.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.290
So we started researching
what different things were

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:23.610
out there.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:24.940
And we came across BigQuery.

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.390
And BigQuery had a lot of
benefits towards GIS data

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:29.420
specifically.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.020
So all of this location
data-- a lot of the analysis

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:33.460
and a lot of the
complex analytics

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.130
really require standard
geography libraries.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:38.830
And so what Google has
put into BigQuery there

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.590
for standard geography was a
really strong differentiator

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:42.310
for us.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:44.930
And then the general support
we saw from the Google teams--

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.963
so when we engaged Google, they
were very good about providing

00:05:47.963 --> 00:05:50.380
specialists who would help us
with architecture, who would

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.080
help our teams, give us
training, really get us up

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:54.350
to speed and comfortable.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.430
And finally, the fact that
BigQuery runs off of ANSI SQL

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:59.620
meant that a lot of the
stuff we'd built in the past

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:02.530
could be ripped out of the
system it was in and placed up

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:05.020
into the cloud quite quickly
and quite successfully.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.880
And we saw that when we first
migrated, about a year ago,

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:08.980
a large chunk of our data.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.740
And we were able to move some
of our algorithms within days

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.420
and got some very fast results.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.310
I think we had a
25-times speed increase.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.720
So what was taking a month
was taking a day to run.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:23.620
So it's a significant difference
in just that caliber of result.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.100
And with that, we're also
able to really, really,

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:30.130
truly use our human capital
as well as possible.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:32.020
So part of what we're
running is consulting.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.720
And you ideally want to reduce
the number of man hours taken

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.915
because, when I'm sending to
pay a data scientist to watch

00:06:37.915 --> 00:06:40.600
a circle spin on their screen
while Hadoop's chugging away

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.640
at a result, I'm still
paying for their time

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:44.870
whether or not that
result's coming through.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.450
And so BigQuery
has also allowed us

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.920
to do more with a small team.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:49.480
And we couldn't
have done the things

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.320
we've done around COVID
without the technology there.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.110
BRUNO AZIZA: We hear this a lot
from customers, is typically,

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:57.940
big data yields
to big questions.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.810
But the ability to go
through the cycle faster

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.600
enables you to ask bigger
questions but also maybe better

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.370
questions and maybe
more questions.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.550
So it invites people
to innovate faster

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:11.050
without having to worry so much
about the operational aspect

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:11.550
of it.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.950
Now, you have learned a ton
through your experience.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:16.660
And I want to know--
first, let's start

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.730
with the positive
side of the equation.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:22.810
As you're learning what it means
to become data-driven and be

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.850
modernized in the
cloud, what are

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:27.100
some of the, maybe, two,
three best practices

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.170
you've learned in this process?

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:30.790
JOE BETTRIDGE: I
think the number one

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.670
best practice is to keep
on top of what's happening.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.400
You guys are launching
new tools and new ways

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.400
of looking at data,
new pieces, constantly.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:40.870
And the rollout is quick.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.250
There's new stuff happening.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.022
And keeping on top of that can
really save you a lot of time.

00:07:45.022 --> 00:07:46.480
We found that
sometimes it's faster

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:48.272
to come to the Google
product team and say,

00:07:48.272 --> 00:07:50.200
hey, we really want
to be able to do X.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.940
And oftentimes, you guys
are already working on that

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.565
and can give us a
date when it's ready.

00:07:53.565 --> 00:07:56.220
So it's faster than us building
it from scratch ourselves

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.370
and then having to replace
it a few months later.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:00.910
So really, keeping on
top of update logs,

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.372
being involved in
community forums,

00:08:02.372 --> 00:08:04.330
understanding what's
happening with the product

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.520
and how it's evolving--
that's a really big

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.340
do and has saved us a lot of
headaches and a lot of time.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.350
Getting close to the
Google team kind of rolls

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.290
into that-- but understanding
what the product team's doing,

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:15.700
being close to
your sales agents,

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.740
understanding what
they're providing

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.060
and what they can help with.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:20.847
The team has been
incredibly supportive.

00:08:20.847 --> 00:08:22.930
It's been one of the better
experiences we've had.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:25.138
I've been in the software
industry for about 10 years

00:08:25.138 --> 00:08:27.190
now, and I would say
it's second to none

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.440
in terms of the quality
of service we've received.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.933
So really, really
happy with that.

00:08:30.933 --> 00:08:32.350
And I think the
closer you can get

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.350
with the people you're
working with from Google,

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:35.799
the more you can gain from that.

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:38.260
You guys are experts in your
platforms and your tools.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.700
And you're willing to
share that expertise.

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:41.650
And you're very good at
sharing that expertise

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:44.830
in a very genuine way that helps
us move forward as a business.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.380
We've been really
happy with that.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.130
And the third one's
something that I think

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:50.070
is true for any data
science team, regardless

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.070
of where you're working.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.260
But give people the
opportunity to experiment.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.720
Things don't always work
the same in BigQuery

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.440
as traditional
database technologies.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.650
Things don't always do
what you want them to do.

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:01.750
You really want to give
your people enough time

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.020
to experiment and play and
do R&amp;D work if you really

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.940
want to see the true
success and drive

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.140
those really big measures.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.330
If you don't give
people time to explore,

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:12.580
they won't flourish as
much as they really can

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:13.960
with these technologies.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:15.460
There's a bit of
a paradigm shift

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.127
in terms of the
thinking of how you have

00:09:17.127 --> 00:09:19.765
to look at data in BigQuery
and how you want to query it,

00:09:19.765 --> 00:09:20.890
how you want to look at it.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.300
And giving people time to
understand that, play with it,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.870
and experiment can really lead
to some astonishing results.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.090
It can really improve how
you're working with it.

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.060
As much as things are ANSI SQL,
and you can rip and replace,

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:32.800
you can do significantly
better if you

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:34.510
take the time to look
at how to optimize

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:35.843
and how to play around with it.

00:09:35.843 --> 00:09:37.510
BRUNO AZIZA: Thank
you for the feedback.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:38.650
We're happy that you're happy.

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.442
And it's true-- if
you're in the community,

00:09:40.442 --> 00:09:42.640
we have roles dedicated
to your success,

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:48.140
from our customer engineers
through our developer relations

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:48.640
team.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.050
And we have people
really focused

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.850
on making sure
you're successful.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:55.320
I'm assuming you're also reading
newsletters and other forums.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.890
But what's your favorite forum?

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:57.850
I'm curious.

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:00.250
JOE BETTRIDGE: So Chad
from your GIS group

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.190
has a community
forum on Google Chat.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:04.840
He set up a really
great forum there

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.413
that has a lot of the leading
people in the community--

00:10:07.413 --> 00:10:09.580
and being able to see what
people are posting about,

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.860
see messaging, really
get on top of that.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.782
And the other thing is
the sales engineers--

00:10:13.782 --> 00:10:15.240
we have a lot of
meetings with them

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.030
to regularly keep up to date on
what products are coming out,

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.170
what's new and exciting.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.927
And I find both of
those are the best

00:10:20.927 --> 00:10:23.010
ways of getting information
for me because they're

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:24.915
very knowledgeable
on the product,

00:10:24.915 --> 00:10:27.540
more so than what I've seen from
most sales engineering groups.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.060
They really know what's
coming up and what it will do.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.615
And they also taken the time
to understand our problems

00:10:32.615 --> 00:10:33.990
and know where
we're coming from.

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:36.120
So they know if a product's
a good fit for us.

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.260
And they're often very
on top of saying, hey, we

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.510
have this new thing launching.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.320
You should check it out.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.112
And they know who to
go to within our team.

00:10:42.112 --> 00:10:44.850
So they've been really,
really good to work with.

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:45.540
BRUNO AZIZA: That's
great to hear.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.415
And yeah, this community
you're talking about

00:10:47.415 --> 00:10:50.370
is what we call the DACC,
the Data Analytics Customer

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:52.920
Council, which is a
meeting that we have.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:54.360
We used to have
this once a year,

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.793
but now we have
this every month.

00:10:55.793 --> 00:10:57.210
So it gives you
more opportunities

00:10:57.210 --> 00:11:00.840
to connect with our roadmap
and our developer organization,

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:01.890
engineering organization.

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.510
And then, of course,
there is a group

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:05.385
where you don't have to
wait for the meeting.

00:11:05.385 --> 00:11:06.800
You can interact
with us directly

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.050
and the rest of the community.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.020
That's what it means to be
part of the data analytics

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:10.960
community at Google.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.110
So now, that was
for the positive.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.990
Let's talk about some
of the worse practices,

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.940
some of, maybe, the
mistakes that you've

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.850
learned along the way that
you would tell someone getting

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.507
started today to
try to avoid them.

00:11:22.507 --> 00:11:23.340
JOE BETTRIDGE: Yeah.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.710
The number one mistake
is, know what data

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.050
you have duplicated where,
how things are set up

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.660
in terms of retention policies.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.150
We made a stupid mistake
of, one of our teams

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:37.170
had accidentally set a default
to retain data for three years

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.100
after deletion.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.600
So we thought we
were deleting data.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.430
And we're watching our
bills go up and up and up

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:42.490
and couldn't figure it out.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:43.090
We reached out.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:44.190
We submitted support tickets.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.273
And someone from Google
realized what was going on

00:11:46.273 --> 00:11:47.370
and corrected it for us.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.920
But it really taught
us a big lesson about,

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:51.810
cloud expenses can go way up.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.450
I mean, relative to the
human capital costs, again,

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.210
that we were seen before
on our old infrastructure,

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.310
this just pales in comparison.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.560
We really didn't pay nearly
as much for these mistakes

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.685
as we were paying to have
people sit at a computer.

00:12:03.685 --> 00:12:05.280
So it was a good
lesson to learn.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.010
But I would suggest people
are careful with things

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:09.120
like retention policies.

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.280
Make sure you have the right
data in the right environments.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:13.590
Don't overreplicate your
data if you don't need to.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.720
Use things like views
to allow data to be

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.550
accessed across environments.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:19.800
Use the tools that are
there to be successful.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.553
It's not traditional
database management.

00:12:21.553 --> 00:12:23.470
You have to treat it a
little bit differently.

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:24.887
So that would be
my number one one

00:12:24.887 --> 00:12:27.667
because that has some
cost associated with it.

00:12:27.667 --> 00:12:29.250
And it rolls into
my second one, which

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.620
is, really, don't be
afraid to fail at this.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:32.885
Things are going to go wrong.

00:12:32.885 --> 00:12:34.260
But you'll be able
to crack them.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.850
You'll be able to
stand it back up.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.770
Again, we launched
a major product

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:40.920
in three weeks that was
meeting a very serious need

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.780
in the market for
data around COVID.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.690
It's not something we could have
done on our old infrastructure.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.240
A lot of us spent some very late
nights in those three weeks,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:49.270
getting that up and running.

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:51.690
It was probably closer to six
to eight weeks of actual work.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:53.107
But still, it's
something that can

00:12:53.107 --> 00:12:55.560
be done with this kind
of tool behind you.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.148
And you can't do that on
traditional, on-prem things.

00:12:58.148 --> 00:12:59.940
Just ordering hardware
alone would take you

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:00.850
longer than that.

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.030
So really, the power is there.

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:04.560
It just needs to be utilized.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.940
And you have to be not afraid
to take that first step.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:08.400
And I think a lot of
people are afraid to take

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.840
that first step because
they might get it wrong.

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.270
And it's not a big deal because
things are moving faster.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:13.320
You get something wrong--

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:14.910
you can course
correct much easier

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.610
than you could in traditional
hardware-type database

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:18.370
development.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:20.730
So the other one is, don't
expect this to magically fix

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:21.300
everything.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.210
I think some of our
team, especially

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:25.710
at our executive level, were
expecting, you go to the cloud,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:27.335
and suddenly, there's
no more problems.

00:13:27.335 --> 00:13:30.330
Everything's better-- brush your
hands, walk away kind of thing.

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.710
And it's really not that.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:33.420
This will fix some
of your problems.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.380
It will introduce
some new problems.

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:36.810
You still need
the support teams.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.240
You still need the
infrastructure people there.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.340
Security development
still needs to be there.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:43.260
Their roles might
shift slightly in what

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.610
they're doing versus managing
services versus managing

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:46.110
hardware.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:48.150
But they're still needed, and
they're still a necessary part

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:49.068
of those teams.

00:13:49.068 --> 00:13:51.360
You're not going to save
costs on those kind of things.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.520
But where you will save costs
are on the actual people

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:54.210
doing the work.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:57.000
And you'll be able to do more
work with less people, which

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.500
is really a bigger benefit.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:01.680
But don't expect just suddenly
to be one data scientist

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.210
spinning up an
entire environment,

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.610
fixing everything and solving
all the problems for you.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.890
You're going to need teams to
support these kind of things.

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:09.682
And that's something
that's just necessary,

00:14:09.682 --> 00:14:11.030
regardless of where you go.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.113
BRUNO AZIZA: That's great.

00:14:12.113 --> 00:14:13.515
You gave us a lot
to think about.

00:14:13.515 --> 00:14:14.640
I mean, there's a lot here.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.440
It's a very rich interview.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.670
Thanks for giving
us the time today.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.180
I have two things I think
I want to highlight--

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:25.440
this first aspect of staying
in touch with the Google team,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.630
either through the
field resources you have

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:30.120
for your account or the
many opportunities--

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.640
forums and newsletters
and groups--

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:33.960
that we have created for you.

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:35.160
I really want to
encourage everyone

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:37.200
to do that if you're part
of the community today.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.783
And then the second
aspect you touched

00:14:38.783 --> 00:14:42.000
on on financial governance-- you
have to watch out for how you

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:43.442
set up your system, of course.

00:14:43.442 --> 00:14:45.150
But the good news here
is, because you're

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.802
in the cloud, it's more than
just your team watching out,

00:14:47.802 --> 00:14:50.010
which I think is what you're
talking about here, Joe,

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.070
is that we managed to pick that
up and then tell you about it,

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.480
which is a great
way to make sure

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.070
that everybody's
paying attention

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:59.400
to how you're utilizing
and paying for resources.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:01.233
Again, thank you so
much for the time today.

00:15:01.233 --> 00:15:03.067
I hope people are going
to reach out to you.

00:15:03.067 --> 00:15:04.620
You've got a lot
to share, and we

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.270
appreciate you taking the time.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:07.260
I'll talk to you soon.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:07.650
JOE BETTRIDGE: Perfect.

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:08.567
Thanks so much, Bruno.

00:15:08.567 --> 00:15:09.330
Take care.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:12.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

